 | Harness fixed cost: the per-shard cost outside the entries themselves (worktrees, baseline builds, baseline checks; the harness prints it as "fixed cost", slowest worker). P4-12 narrowed what each entry builds and barely moved it, 516 s to 463 s on GitHub's slowest shard, so another per-entry optimisation is the wrong lever. **The measurement to beat** (run 36819512559, P4-12's tooling, first run): the tightest harness clock was 758 s (12.6 min) of the 900 s ceiling, `5acbd0d` shard 7, of which 464 s was fixed cost. The slowest per-commit job step was 13.8 min (`032a0dd` shard 1), but 2.8 min of it was preflight, build, gates and tests, outside the harness clock. The two designs to choose between when it comes due: (a) one baseline build shared by every worker's worktree, each worktree rebuilding only what its patch touches; (b) a persistent pool of worktrees, built once per shard and reset between entries. First step either way: the harness prints the fixed cost split into worktrees, baseline builds and baseline checks. That split has not been measured, and if baseline checks dominate (the `Raft.Scale.Tests` suite took 301 s on GitHub in the same run), neither design is the lever (reviewer, at P4 acceptance) | P6 | open | — |
